Below is a **complete, working example** that shows how to

1. **Load a PMTiles file** (a single-file container of vector tiles) with the official `pmtiles` library,  
2. **Add it as a vector source** to a MapLibre GL JS v6 map, and  
3. **Draw a line layer** from one of the source’s layers.

The code works both when you bundle with a tool like Vite/webpack **and** when you load the map from a CDN.  
All the v6-specific migration points (ESM-only build, no default export, `setWorkerUrl()`, etc.) are highlighted.

---

## 1️⃣ Install the packages (bundler-only)

```bash
# npm or yarn – pick the version you need
npm i maplibre-gl@^6 pmtiles
# or
yarn add maplibre-gl@^6 pmtiles
```

> **If you are using a CDN** you can skip the install step – the same code works with the CDN URLs shown later.

---

## 2️⃣ Bundle-friendly version (Vite / webpack / Rollup …)

```ts
// src/main.ts --------------------------------------------------------------
import 'maplibre-gl/dist/maplibre-gl.css';               // map styles
import { Map, setWorkerUrl } from 'maplibre-gl';          // named import – no default export
import { PMTiles } from 'pmtiles';

// ---------------------------------------------------------------
// 2️⃣1️⃣ Tell MapLibre where its WebWorker lives (required for bundlers)
import workerUrl from 'maplibre-gl/dist/maplibre-gl-worker.mjs?worker&url';
setWorkerUrl(workerUrl);

// ---------------------------------------------------------------
// 2️⃣2️⃣ Register the `pmtiles://` protocol handler
// Replace the URL with the location of your .pmtiles file (can be relative or absolute)
const pmtilesUrl = new URL('./data/my-tiles.pmtiles', import.meta.url).toString();
const pm = new PMTiles(pmtilesUrl);
pm.addProtocol();   // <-- this installs a fetch handler for the `pmtiles://` scheme

// ---------------------------------------------------------------
// 2️⃣3️⃣ Create the map
const map = new Map({
  container: 'map',               // <div id="map"></div> in your HTML
  style: 'https://demotiles.maplibre.org/style.json', // any base style you like
  center: [0, 0],
  zoom: 2,
});

// ---------------------------------------------------------------
// 2️⃣4️⃣ When the style is ready, add the PMTiles source + line layer
map.on('style.load', () => {
  // ---- vector source -------------------------------------------------
  map.addSource('my-pmtiles-source', {
    type: 'vector',
    // The URL must use the `pmtiles://` scheme; the part after the scheme
    // must be **exactly the same string you passed to `new PMTiles()`**.
    url: `pmtiles://${pmtilesUrl}`,
  });

  // ---- line layer ----------------------------------------------------
  // Replace `my_line_layer` with the actual source-layer name that lives
  // inside the PMTiles file (you can inspect it with `pmtiles info …` or
  // with a tool like TileServer-GL).
  map.addLayer({
    id: 'my-lines',
    type: 'line',
    source: 'my-pmtiles-source',
    'source-layer': 'my_line_layer',   // <-- name inside the vector tiles
    paint: {
      'line-color': '#ff6600',
      'line-width': 3,
      'line-opacity': 0.9,
    },
  });
});
```

### What the code does

| Step | Why it matters for v6 |
|------|-----------------------|
| `import { Map, setWorkerUrl } from 'maplibre-gl'` | v6 has **no default export** – you must use a named or namespace import. |
| `setWorkerUrl(workerUrl)` | Bundlers cannot resolve the worker automatically; v6 requires a one-time call. |
| `new PMTiles(...).addProtocol()` | Registers a **fetch handler** for the `pmtiles://` scheme. Without this the browser would try a normal HTTP fetch and fail. |
| `url: \`pmtiles://${pmtilesUrl}\`` | The source URL must use the `pmtiles://` protocol; the string after the scheme must match the URL you gave to `PMTiles`. |
| `map.on('style.load', …)` | Guarantees the base style is loaded before we add our custom source/layer. |

---

## 3️⃣ CDN-only version (no bundler)

If you prefer a single HTML file with no build step, just swap the imports for CDN URLs and **omit** the `setWorkerUrl()` call (the CDN ESM build can locate its worker automatically).

```html
<!DOCTYPE html>
<html lang="en">
<head>
  <meta charset="UTF-8">
  <title>MapLibre + PMTiles (CDN)</title>
  <link href="https://unpkg.com/maplibre-gl@^6/dist/maplibre-gl.css" rel="stylesheet">
  <style>
    #map { position:absolute; top:0; bottom:0; left:0; right:0; }
  </style>
</head>
<body>
<div id="map"></div>

<script type="module">
  // -------------------------------------------------------------
  // 1️⃣ Import the ESM builds from the CDN
  import * as maplibregl from 'https://unpkg.com/maplibre-gl@^6/dist/maplibre-gl.mjs';
  import { PMTiles } from 'https://cdn.jsdelivr.net/npm/pmtiles@latest/dist/index.mjs';

  // -------------------------------------------------------------
  // 2️⃣ Register the pmtiles protocol
  const pmtilesUrl = 'https://example.com/path/to/tiles.pmtiles'; // <-- change this
  const pm = new PMTiles(pmtilesUrl);
  pm.addProtocol();

  // -------------------------------------------------------------
  // 3️⃣ Create the map
  const map = new maplibregl.Map({
    container: 'map',
    style: 'https://demotiles.maplibre.org/style.json',
    center: [0, 0],
    zoom: 2,
  });

  // -------------------------------------------------------------
  // 4️⃣ Add source + line layer once the style is ready
  map.on('style.load', () => {
    map.addSource('my-pmtiles-source', {
      type: 'vector',
      url: `pmtiles://${pmtilesUrl}`,
    });

    map.addLayer({
      id: 'my-lines',
      type: 'line',
      source: 'my-pmtiles-source',
      'source-layer': 'my_line_layer',   // <-- adjust to your data
      paint: {
        'line-color': '#0066ff',
        'line-width': 2,
        'line-opacity': 0.8,
      },
    });
  });
</script>
</body>
</html>
```

**Key differences from the bundler version**

| CDN | Bundler |
|-----|---------|
| No `setWorkerUrl()` – the ESM build auto-detects the worker via `import.meta.url`. | Must call `setWorkerUrl()` once (see the Vite snippet above). |
| Imports are absolute URLs (`type="module"`). | Imports are resolved by the bundler; you can use the normal `import … from 'maplibre-gl'`. |
| The `pmtiles` library is also loaded from a CDN (`jsdelivr` in the example). | Installed from npm (`pmtiles`). |

---

## 4️⃣ Quick checklist – “Did I miss anything?”  

| ✅ | Item |
|----|------|
| **ESM import** – `import * as maplibregl` or named imports (`{ Map }`). No default export. |
| **Worker URL** – `setWorkerUrl()` **only** when you bundle (Vite, webpack, etc.). |
| **PMTiles protocol** – `new PMTiles(url).addProtocol();` must run **before** you add the source. |
| **Source URL** – `pmtiles://<exact-same-string-you-passed-to-PMTiles>` (including scheme, host, query, etc.). |
| **Source-layer name** – use the exact layer name that exists inside the vector tiles (you can inspect it with `pmtiles info <file>` or by loading the tiles in a viewer). |
| **Style load** – add the source/layer after `style.load` (or `map.on('load')` if you don’t change the style later). |
| **v6 API** – use public getters (`map.getZoom()`, `map.getCenter()`, …) – `map.transform` no longer exists. |
| **TypeScript** – if you type the event handler, use `MapSourceDataEvent | MapStyleDataEvent` instead of the removed `MapDataEvent`. |

---

## 5️⃣ TL;DR – Minimal copy-paste snippet (bundler)

```ts
import { Map, setWorkerUrl } from 'maplibre-gl';
import workerUrl from 'maplibre-gl/dist/maplibre-gl-worker.mjs?worker&url';
import { PMTiles } from 'pmtiles';
import 'maplibre-gl/dist/maplibre-gl.css';

setWorkerUrl(workerUrl);

const pm = new PMTiles('/data/roads.pmtiles');
pm.addProtocol();

const map = new Map({
  container: 'map',
  style: 'https://demotiles.maplibre.org/style.json',
  center: [0, 0],
  zoom: 2,
});

map.on('style.load', () => {
  map.addSource('roads', {
    type: 'vector',
    url: `pmtiles://${pm.url}`,   // same string you gave to PMTiles
  });

  map.addLayer({
    id: 'roads-line',
    type: 'line',
    source: 'roads',
    'source-layer': 'road_lines', // <-- adjust
    paint: {
      'line-color': '#ff0000',
      'line-width': 2,
    },
  });
});
```

---

### 🎉 You’re done!

*Open the page, you should see the line features from your PMTiles file rendered on top of the base style.*  
If nothing appears:

1. Open the browser console – look for `404` or `ERR_PACKAGE_PATH_NOT_EXPORTED` errors.  
2. Verify the **source-layer name** matches what’s inside the PMTiles file.  
3. Make sure the **PMTiles URL** is reachable (CORS headers may be needed).  

Happy mapping! 🚀

Below is a **complete, working example** that shows how to

1. load a **PMTiles** file (a single-file MBTiles/PMTiles archive) in a MapLibre GL JS v6 map,  
2. add it as a **vector-tile source**, and  
3. draw a **line layer** from one of the source’s layers.

The code works both when you load MapLibre from a CDN **or** when you bundle it with a tool like Vite/webpack.  
All the v6-specific migration points (named/namespace imports, no default export, `setWorkerUrl()` for bundlers, etc.) are highlighted.

---

## 1️⃣ Install the required packages

```bash
# If you are using a bundler (Vite, webpack, etc.)
npm i maplibre-gl @protomaps/pmtiles
# or with Yarn / pnpm …
```

> **NOTE** – `@protomaps/pmtiles` is a tiny helper that registers a custom `pmtiles://` protocol so MapLibre can fetch tiles directly from the file without a separate server.

---

## 2️⃣ CDN-only version (no bundler)

```html
<!DOCTYPE html>
<html lang="en">
<head>
  <meta charset="UTF-8">
  <title>PMTiles + MapLibre v6</title>
  <link href="https://unpkg.com/maplibre-gl@^6.0.0/dist/maplibre-gl.css" rel="stylesheet"/>
  <style>
    #map { position:absolute; top:0; bottom:0; left:0; right:0; }
  </style>
</head>
<body>
<div id="map"></div>

<script type="module">
  // -------------------------------------------------
  // 1️⃣ Import the ESM-only build of MapLibre
  // -------------------------------------------------
  import * as maplibregl from 'https://unpkg.com/maplibre-gl@^6.0.0/dist/maplibre-gl.mjs';
  // -------------------------------------------------
  // 2️⃣ Import the PMTiles helper (also ESM)
  // -------------------------------------------------
  import { PMTiles } from 'https://cdn.jsdelivr.net/npm/@protomaps/pmtiles@latest/dist/index.mjs';

  // -------------------------------------------------
  // 3️⃣ Register the custom protocol
  // -------------------------------------------------
  // The URL you pass to `new PMTiles()` can be a relative path,
  // an absolute URL, or a data-uri.  Here we assume the file lives
  // next to this HTML file.
  const pmtiles = new PMTiles('my-data.pmtiles');

  // Tell the browser’s fetch API how to resolve `pmtiles://…` URLs.
  // The callback receives a Request object and must return a Response.
  maplibregl.addProtocol('pmtiles', (request) => pmtiles.get(request));

  // -------------------------------------------------
  // 4️⃣ Create the map
  // -------------------------------------------------
  const map = new maplibregl.Map({
    container: 'map',
    style: {
      version: 8,
      sources: {},
      layers: []   // we’ll add our own source & layer later
    },
    center: [0, 0],
    zoom: 2
  });

  // -------------------------------------------------
  // 5️⃣ When the style is ready, add the vector source
  // -------------------------------------------------
  map.on('style.load', () => {
    // The URL uses the custom protocol we just registered.
    map.addSource('my-pmtiles-source', {
      type: 'vector',
      // The part after `pmtiles://` is just an identifier – it can be
      // anything you like, as long as it matches the request the
      // protocol handler receives.
      url: 'pmtiles://my-data.pmtiles'
    });

    // -------------------------------------------------
    // 6️⃣ Add a line layer that draws from a source-layer
    // -------------------------------------------------
    // Replace `my_line_layer` with the actual layer name that lives
    // inside the PMTiles file (you can inspect it with `pmtiles info`
    // or any MBTiles viewer).
    map.addLayer({
      id: 'my-lines',
      type: 'line',
      source: 'my-pmtiles-source',
      'source-layer': 'my_line_layer',   // <-- change this!
      paint: {
        'line-color': '#ff6600',
        'line-width': 2,
        'line-opacity': 0.8
      }
    });
  });
</script>
</body>
</html>
```

### What changed compared to a v5 example?

| v5 pattern | v6 replacement |
|------------|----------------|
| `<script src="…/dist/maplibre-gl.js"></script>` | `<script type="module"> import … from '…/dist/maplibre-gl.mjs'; </script>` |
| `import maplibregl from 'maplibre-gl'` | `import * as maplibregl from 'maplibre-gl'` (or named imports) |
| `require('maplibre-gl')` | Not supported – use dynamic `import()` if you must stay in CommonJS. |
| No worker-URL handling needed for CDN | Same – the CDN ESM build auto-detects the worker. |

---

## 3️⃣ Bundler version (Vite, webpack, esbuild, …)

Below is a minimal **Vite** setup; the same ideas apply to webpack, Rollup, etc.

### `index.html`

```html
<!DOCTYPE html>
<html lang="en">
<head>
  <meta charset="UTF-8">
  <title>PMTiles + MapLibre (bundler)</title>
  <link rel="stylesheet" href="/node_modules/maplibre-gl/dist/maplibre-gl.css">
</head>
<body>
  <div id="map" style="position:absolute; inset:0;"></div>
  <script type="module" src="/src/main.ts"></script>
</body>
</html>
```

### `src/main.ts`

```ts
// -------------------------------------------------
// 1️⃣ Named imports – no default export in v6
// -------------------------------------------------
import { Map, setWorkerUrl, addProtocol } from 'maplibre-gl';
import 'maplibre-gl/dist/maplibre-gl.css';

// -------------------------------------------------
// 2️⃣ PMTiles helper (ESM)
// -------------------------------------------------
import { PMTiles } from '@protomaps/pmtiles';

// -------------------------------------------------
// 3️⃣ Tell MapLibre where the worker lives (required for bundlers)
// -------------------------------------------------
import workerUrl from 'maplibre-gl/dist/maplibre-gl-worker.mjs?worker&url';
setWorkerUrl(workerUrl);

// -------------------------------------------------
// 4️⃣ Register the custom protocol
// -------------------------------------------------
const pmtiles = new PMTiles(new URL('./data/my-data.pmtiles', import.meta.url).toString());

// `addProtocol` is a static helper exported by MapLibre v6.
// It registers a fetch-handler for the `pmtiles://` scheme.
addProtocol('pmtiles', (request) => pmtiles.get(request));

// -------------------------------------------------
// 5️⃣ Create the map
// -------------------------------------------------
const map = new Map({
  container: 'map',
  style: {
    version: 8,
    sources: {},
    layers: []
  },
  center: [0, 0],
  zoom: 2
});

// -------------------------------------------------
// 6️⃣ Add source + line layer once the style is ready
// -------------------------------------------------
map.on('style.load', () => {
  map.addSource('my-pmtiles-source', {
    type: 'vector',
    url: 'pmtiles://my-data.pmtiles'   // matches the protocol handler above
  });

  map.addLayer({
    id: 'my-lines',
    type: 'line',
    source: 'my-pmtiles-source',
    'source-layer': 'my_line_layer',   // <-- change to your actual layer name
    paint: {
      'line-color': '#0066ff',
      'line-width': 3,
      'line-opacity': 0.9
    }
  });
});
```

#### Why the `setWorkerUrl()` call?

* In a **bundler** the worker file (`maplibre-gl-worker.mjs`) is no longer reachable via `import.meta.url`.  
* `setWorkerUrl()` tells MapLibre the exact URL of that worker.  
* The `?worker&url` query is the Vite-recommended way; other bundlers use a similar pattern (`new URL('…/maplibre-gl-worker.mjs', import.meta.url).toString()`).

---

## 4️⃣ Quick checklist

| ✅ Done? | Item |
|--------|------|
| ✅ Imported the **ESM-only** MapLibre build (`import * as maplibregl …` or named imports). |
| ✅ No default export – used namespace or named imports. |
| ✅ If you are bundling, called `setWorkerUrl()` **once** before creating the `Map`. |
| ✅ Registered a `pmtiles://` protocol via `addProtocol` (or `maplibregl.addProtocol` for CDN). |
| ✅ Added a **vector** source that points at the PMTiles file. |
| ✅ Added a **line** layer that references the correct `source-layer` name. |
| ✅ Styled the line with `line-color`, `line-width`, etc. |
| ✅ Tested that the map renders (no blank map, no worker-load errors). |

---

## 5️⃣ Gotchas & troubleshooting

| Symptom | Likely cause | Fix |
|---------|--------------|-----|
| **Blank map, console shows `Failed to load worker`** | Bundler but `setWorkerUrl()` missing or wrong URL. | Add the `setWorkerUrl()` snippet shown above. |
| **`ERR_PACKAGE_PATH_NOT_EXPORTED`** | You are still using `require('maplibre-gl')` in a plain Node script. | Switch the file to ESM (`.mjs` or `"type":"module"` in `package.json`) and use `import { Map } from 'maplibre-gl'`. |
| **Missing icons / sprites** | Your style references `icon-image` names that aren’t in the sprite sheet. | Use `map.setMissingStyleImageResolver(...)` (v6) instead of the old `styleimagemissing` handler. |
| **`map.transform` is undefined** | You tried to read the internal transform (removed in v6). | Use the public camera getters (`map.getZoom()`, `map.getCenter()`, …). |
| **TypeScript error: `Cannot find name 'MapDataEvent'`** | The old `MapDataEvent` type was removed. | Import `MapSourceDataEvent` / `MapStyleDataEvent` and narrow the union as shown in the migration guide. |

